  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1200 AM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nThe positively tilted upper level trough over the Great Basin \ncontinues to slide east this morning, weakening as it tracks over\nColorado Saturday afternoon. The southwest jet ahead of this wave\nwhich was responsible for the widespread gusty winds Friday \nafternoon also begins to weaken and become more southerly. As \nprevious AFDs have stated, this system will bring another round of\nsnow showers to our mountains, this time favoring the central \nmountains and southern Front Range. We have increased snow \naccumulations a tad to better reflect recent trends, with totals \nnow ranging between 1.0 and 2.0 inches. \n\nThe chance for some snowflakes falling over the Denver metro still \nexists, and as the finer details become more clear, there are a \nfew features that we have been examining. First, although the \ntrough passing over the region looks quite weak, it will still \nbring some favorable ingredients for synoptic ascent. One of \nthese ingredients would be the position of the right-entrance \nregion of an embedded jet streak, favoring ascent over the Denver \nmetro sometime Saturday morning. Other features that would promote\nand/or indicate QG ascent would be the passage of the remaining \nPV anomaly, and strong positive vorticity advection, albeit these \nwill be at their strongest just to our south and east. At the \nsame time, both global model solutions and CAMs suggest an area of\nfrontogenesis at the 700mb level oriented SW-NE, extending from \nthe central mountains and across Denver. This area of \nfrontogenesis could be the key for the development of a more \norganized snowband. At the lowest levels, surface winds Saturday \nmorning are expected to remain light (<8 mph). However, guidance \nhas continued to suggest a broad anticyclonic circulation east of \nDenver, which could help promote some weak upsloping if positioned\nat just the right spot. The most obvious limiting factor appears \nto be moisture, with model soundings showing dry air near the \nsurface that will need to be overcome for snowfall to reach the \nlower elevations. With all of these ingredients in mind, we have \nconcluded that there is about a 30% chance for light snow \naccumulations (up to 0.5 in.) at the lower foothills and east over\nparts of the urban corridor. However, snowfall is likely to occur\nalong a narrow band, meaning that only a small portion of our \narea will end up experiencing the first snowflakes of the season \nat low elevations. We expect this to be a very low-impact event, \nwith a high-end scenario (10% chance) snowfall amount of just \nunder one inch. Snow activity should exit the area by around noon,\nwith decreasing cloud cover and temperatures rising into the \nupper 30s and low 40s for the afternoon. \n\nSunday morning is shaping up to be quite chilly, particularly for \nour mountains and high valleys, where morning lows will dip into the \nteens and single digits under the mostly clear skies. For the Denver \nmetro and plains, morning lows should have no problem dropping into \nthe 20s, with perhaps even some upper teens over the usual cold \nspots. We are monitoring the potential for some freezing fog over \nthe northeast Colorado plains Sunday morning, although reduced \nvisibilities are likely to be the main impact. Afternoon \ntemperatures will be similar to Saturday, generally in the 40s, \ndue to high clouds filling in from the west ahead of the next \ndisturbance. \n\nAnother weakening upper level trough is set to move through the area \nMonday afternoon/evening, bringing another round of 1.0 to 2.0 inch \nsnow totals for our mountains. We are fairly confident in \ntemperatures staying at seasonal averages or slightly below average \nthrough at least Wednesday. However, long-range guidance has trended \nslightly drier and warmer late next week and into next weekend, with \nperhaps only a few shortwave passages that would bring sporadic snow \nchances for the mountains, or a few snowflakes at best for the \nDenver metro and plains.",
